Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 10:21:49 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 17/41] Documentation: tee: say what the copy-through guarantee
 actually is

The security section claimed buffers are copied through kernel-only memory "so
user space cannot change them after validation while the secure world is
reading them". True as a statement about that race, and it reads to anyone
skimming as a much broader containment claim.

It is not containment. The client chooses which offsets to name in patch
descriptors, so it can leave any value it likes at any offset it declines to
patch, and an application is free to treat that as an address. Say so next to
the guarantee rather than several sections later.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
---
 Documentation/tee/qseecom.rst | 6 ++++++
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+)

diff --git a/Documentation/tee/qseecom.rst b/Documentation/tee/qseecom.rst
index dafd85f83..865a0d0f3 100644
--- a/Documentation/tee/qseecom.rst
+++ b/Documentation/tee/qseecom.rst
@@ -56,6 +56,12 @@ Buffers the secure world reads are copied through kernel-only memory, so user
 space cannot change them after validation while the secure world is reading
 them.
 
+That is a narrow guarantee and should not be read as containment. The client
+chooses which offsets to name in patch descriptors, so it can leave any value
+it likes at any offset it declines to patch, and an application is free to
+treat that as an address. The driver validates the patches it is asked to make;
+it cannot validate a protocol it does not know.
+
